\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA MARKETS - Mixed and relaxed about Brazil. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,055 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 1999\par 10:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2     SINGAPORE, Jan 15 (Reuters) - It was a mixed day on Asian \par \par \uc2  markets on Friday in spite of the gloomy news from Brazil, with \par \par \uc2  stock markets also shrugging off a sharp overnight drop on Wall \par \par \uc2  Street. \par \par \uc2      The markets were trading thinly because of a holiday in \par \par \uc2  Japan but the firmer tone of the yen against the dollar helped \par \par \uc2  underpin the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2      Stocks were still the main focus but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders kept a \par \par \uc2  close eye on the equity side of things again to watch for \par \par \uc2  overseas investor cash pulling out of the region. \par \par \uc2      DOLLAR SOFT IN ASIA ON BRAZIL WORRIES \par \par \uc2      The dollar remained on a soft footing as traders began to \par \par \uc2  speculate that Brazil's financial woes might prompt the Federal \par \par \uc2  Reserve to ease U.S. interest rates to cushion the Dow. \par \par \uc2      "There's talk of a possible Fed easing because the U.S. \par \par \uc2  stock market will be hard hit if we see a further impact from \par \par \uc2  Brazil," said a Japanese bank dealer. \par \par \uc2      But some felt the Dow's recent losses might not be a source \par \par \uc2  of great concern to the Fed. \par \par \uc2      "I think they would like to take a little bit of heat out of \par \par \uc2  the Dow. It's been roaring ahead since the start of the year," \par \par \uc2  said Peter Whitley, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at State Bank in \par \par \uc2  Sydney. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN STOCKS GET A BOOST \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's market got a bit of an artificial boost when the \par \par \uc2  government's huge stabilisation fund weighed in towards the end \par \par \uc2  of the session targeting shares which form a large part of the \par \par \uc2  weighted index. These were mainly financial stocks. \par \par \uc2      But bargain hunters were also seen scooping up electronics \par \par \uc2  shares. \par \par \uc2      Taiwan's weighted index finishing up over 3.4 percent for \par \par \uc2  the day. \par \par \uc2      KOREA STOCKS STILL DOING WELL BUT HANG SENG WOBBLES \par \par \uc2      In Korea, the KOSPI also had a better session, rising more \par \par \uc2  than 1.5 percent as retail investors took advantage of the lower \par \par \uc2  prices after Thursday's post-Brazil sell-off. \par \par \uc2      In Hong Kong, the market was more worried by developments \par \par \uc2  overseas and shares started off very weak, although in later \par \par \uc2  trading prices recovered most of their losses. \par \par \uc2      SINGAPORE CONFUSION \par \par \uc2      In Southeast Asia, there was some early confusion in \par \par \uc2  Singapore when the Straits Times index plunged 5.5 percent at \par \par \uc2  the opening because of a wrongly keyed transaction. \par \par \uc2      A trader put in a wrong price for OCBC bank and one player \par \par \uc2  bought one lot at 30 cents. \par \par \uc2      It was this error which caused the initial five percent \par \par \uc2  plunge in the index. \par \par \uc2      OCBC was quoted at S$8.25 in late business. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  QUIET AND MIXED \par \par \uc2      It appears Brazil might have done Asia a favour by showing \par \par \uc2  the region is in better shape than Latin America at the moment \par \par \uc2  -- although there are people who think things might take a turn \par \par \uc2  forthe worse at any tie. The strength of the yen pretty much \par \par \uc2  underpinned all the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, with the \par \par \uc2  notable exception of the rupiah. \par \par \uc2      RUPIAH SLIPS GRADUALLY LOWER \par \par \uc2      The rupiah slid past the 9,000 level per dollar at one point \par \par \uc2  on worries about trouble on the streets after a forthcoming \par \par \uc2  public holiday. \par \par \uc2      Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has vowed to step in and defend \par \par \uc2  the rupiah, there has been no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet. \par \par \uc2      Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Sjahril Sabirin has said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2  bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would seek to keep the rupiah firmer than 8,000 to the \par \par \uc2  dollar. Indonesia's budget for the next fiscal year is based on \par \par \uc2  an average rupiah rate of 7,500. \par \par \uc2  But the fear is that when Ramadan ends and the festivities \par \par \uc2  associated with it are over trouble will return to the streets \par \par \uc2  of Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  Students have already pledged to resume mass anti-government \par \par \uc2  protests after the Eid al-Fitr Moslem festival on Tuesday and \par \par \uc2  Wednesday next week. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR REGAINS SOME LOST GROUND \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar managed a small rise as dealers \par \par \uc2  reported an unwinding of the long baht/short Sing positions put \par \par \uc2  on in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  The low level of interest rates in Singapore has prompted \par \par \uc2  investors to borrow Singapore dollars and convert them to other \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and much of this cheap funding went on in Sing/baht. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT EASES \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was easier and back through 37 to the dollar \par \par \uc2  despite news that commercial bank lending is expected to grow \par \par \uc2  seven percent this year after contracting five percent last \par \par \uc2  year. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  Change on the day FRI 15 0843GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...Latest bid....Prev Close....Pct Move \par \par \uc2   Ringgit......3.7997.......3.8000.........0.01% \par \par \uc2   Rupiah......8875.00......8600.00........-3.10% \par \par \uc2   Baht..........37.10........36.90........-0.54% \par \par \uc2   Peso..........38.62........38.48........-0.36% \par \par \uc2   Sing dlr.....1.6795.......1.6840.........0.27% \par \par \uc2   Taiwan dlr...32.165.......32.210.........0.14% \par \par \uc2   Korean won..1182.00......1184.50.........0.21% \par \par \uc2   Japan yen....112.27.......113.85.........1.41% \par \par \uc2   Change since start of Asia crisis \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .....Latest......July '97......Pct Move \par \par \uc2   Ringgit......3.7997.......2.5200........-33.68% \par \par \uc2   Rupiah......8875.00......2432.00........-72.60% \par \par \uc2   Baht..........37.10........25.90........-30.19% \par \par \uc2   Peso..........38.62........26.50........-31.38% \par \par \uc2   Sing dlr.....1.6795.......1.4300........-14.86% \par \par \uc2   Taiwan dlr..32.1650......27.7500........-13.73% \par \par \uc2   Korean won..1182.00.......842.00........-28.76% \par \par \uc2   Japan yen....112.27.......114.40..........1.90% \par \par \uc2  ************************************************ \par \par \uc2   STOCK MARKETS \par \par \uc2   Change on the day FRI 15 0843GMT \par \par \uc2   Market........Current.....Prev Close.....Pct Move \par \par \uc2   Indonesia.....399.10.......408.55.........-2.3% \par \par \uc2   Malaysia......598.06.......593.34..........0.8% \par \par \uc2   Philippines..2041.55......2063.28.........-1.1% \par \par \uc2   Singapore....1494.36......1476.39..........1.2% \pa