\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Rupiah slides on mounting political fears. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Marshall \par \uc2 614 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 1999\par 12:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Jan 13 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah dropped more than five percent against the dollar on Wednesday on regional weakness, fears of fresh domestic unrest and wrangling over the new budget. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah's sudden fall sent tremors through the stock market, where a firmer \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is crucial for scores of debt-ridden firms. The bourse fell 3.92 percent to 419.10 points. \par \par \uc2  By the end of Jakarta trading the rupiah was quoted at 8,550/8,650 to the dollar, after trading as low as 8,650, its lowest in more than two months. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rupiah's problems were set to continue. \par \par \uc2  "Support at 8,600 has been taken, I don't see anything standing in the way of 9,000 now," said one trader at a European bank in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah had a brief respite after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Sjahril Sabirin pledged to step in to help the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recapture the 8,000 level. But there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the decline soon picked up steam again. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rupiah's slide had been sparked by a general weakening in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar as investors cut back on their positions amid concerns about a deepening financial crisis in Brazil. \par \par \uc2  "The initial cause was probably Brazil," one said. "Later, when Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  the rupiah firmed to 8,300, but because there was no action from Bank Indonesia the market recovered its positions." \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts said numerous other worries had contributed to the rupiah's decline. \par \par \uc2  "There have been things building up for a few weeks, and maybe today, given the relatively strong dollar, was the day to take advantage of that," said Clifford Tan, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg Dillon Read in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "The primary concern is about what happens after Ramadan is over. What will happen to the pace and the fervour of political protest across Indonesia? That's issue number one." \par \par \uc2  Indonesian students have vowed to resume mass anti-government rallies after the Eid al-Fitr festival on Tuesday and Wednesday which marks the end of the Moslem fasting month of Ramadan. \par \par \uc2  A series of bloody clashes between security forces and students came to a halt last month when students stopped their protests for the Moslem fasting month. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a battle between the government and parliament over Indonesia's 1999/2000 budget had also rattled markets. \par \par \uc2  Parliament said this week the government should cut the amount of money it is spending on the crucial bank recapitalisation scheme and revise some budget estimates. \par \par \uc2  "The parliamentary infighting over the budget - that's new for Indonesia, given that it was a rubber-stamp body in the past," Tan said. "In other countries like Mexico the political cost of paying for these large recapitalisations has often become quite a large issue." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said concerns that the beleaguered armed forces could disintegrate was also weighing on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia has been converting $5-15 million a day of aid and loan money into rupiah in recent months, giving a significant boost to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the thin market meant Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be relatively effective, but warned the funds to be converted into rupiah could soon start drying up. \par \par \uc2  "They have to (defend the rupiah), but we think that the trend is going to be more for a depreciation," said Mangal Goswami, economist at ABN AMRO in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "Official aid money is going to taper off, the current account is going to fall.... These are all factors that are going to go against the rupiah." \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1d06qpd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
