\par\par \b \uc2  Rupiah tumbles more than five pct against dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 717 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 1999\par 11:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Jan 13 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah dived more than five percent against the dollar on Wednesday, breaking through support at 8,600 on regional weakness, political concerns and activity in the swaps market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Support at 8,600 has been taken, I don't see anything standing in the way of 9,000 now," said one trader at a European bank in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  By 0850 GMT the rupiah was quoted at 8,575/8,625 after trading as low as 8,650. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said comments by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it would step in to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falling through 8,000 briefly helped the rupiah but there had been no sign of Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline had picked up steam again. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rupiah's slide had been sparked by a general weakening in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar as investors cut back on their positions amid concerns about a deepening financial crisis in Brazil. \par \par \uc2  "The initial cause was probably Brazil," one said "Later when Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  the rupiah firmed to 8,300, but because there was no action from Bank Indonesia the market recovered its positions." \par \par \uc2  He said activity in the swaps market suggested there was more rupiah weakness to come: "It may continue because if you look at the swaps it looks like overseas players are building up long positions. So they might be attacking the rupiah again." \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts said numerous other worries had also contributed to the rupiah's decline. \par \par \uc2  "There have been things building up for a few weeks, and maybe today, given the relatively strong dollar, was the day to take advantage of that," said Clifford Tan, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg Dillon Read in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "The primary concern is about what happens after Ramadan is over. What will happen to the pace and the fervour of political protest across Indonesia? That's issue number one." \par \par \uc2  Indonesian students have vowed to resume mass anti-government rallies after the Eid al-Fitr festival on Tuesday and Wednesday which marks the end of the Moslem fasting month of Ramadan. \par \par \uc2  A series of bloody clashes between security forces and students came to a halt last month when students stopped their protests for the Moslem fasting month. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a political battle between the government and parliament over Indonesia's 1999/2000 budget had also rattled markets. Parliament said this week the government should cut the amount of money it is spending on the crucial bank recapitalisation scheme and revise some budget estimates. \par \par \uc2  "The parliamentary infighting over the budget - that's new for Indonesia, given that it was a rubber-stamp body in the past," Tan said. \par \par \uc2  "In other countries like Mexico the political cost of paying for these large recapitalisations has often become quite a large issue." \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia Governor Syahril Sabirin said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would step in to try to keep the rupiah firmer than 8,000 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been converting $5 million to $15 million a day in aid and loan dollars to rupiah in recent months, providing a significant boost for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in thin market conditions. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the thin market meant Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be relatively effective, but warned the funds to be converted into rupiah could soon start drying up. \par \par \uc2  "They have to (defend the rupiah), but we think that the trend is going to be more for a depreciation," said Mangal Goswami, economist at ABN AMRO in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "Official aid money is going to taper off, the current account is going to fall.... These are all factors that are going to go against the rupiah." \par \par \uc2  He said ABN AMRO forecast the rupiah dropping to 9,000 in March and 10,000 in June, before returning to 9,000 in September. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia's budget for the fiscal year to end-March 2000 assumes an average exchange rate of 7,500 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The real level is 8,000 to 10,000," one senior foreign bank dealer said. "Below 8,000 is unrealistic due to the political outlook." \par \par \uc2  ((Andrew Marshall, Jakarta newsroom +6221 384-6364; Fax +6221 344-8404, jakarta.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1d06qck\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
