\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Jakarta markets grind to a halt amid clashes. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Marshall \par \uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 1998\par 13:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Nov 13 (Reuters) - Indonesia's financial markets ground to a halt on Friday as violent clashes in the heart of Jakarta's business district left at least three people dead and virtually shut down trading in stocks and the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Most bank offices were deserted as police and troops fired rubber bullets, tear gas and bursts from a water cannon to clear more than 10,000 protesters from the central business district. Elsewhere, at least two more people were killed in clashes. \par \par \uc2  The violence had little impact on Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock market but dealers said this was due to a dearth of trade rather than a sign of confidence in the country's fragile political stability. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah ended at 7,800/7,900 to the dollar in Jakarta compared with 7,750/7,850 the day before. The stock market slipped 0.7 percent to 355.54 points. \par \par \uc2  But both markets gained over the week, despite days of mounting tension and clashes as thousands took to the streets to protest against a special session of the country's highest legislative body, the People's Consultative Assembly (MPR). \par \par \uc2  The stock market had ended last Friday at 353.97 points, while the rupiah had closed the week at 8,100. \par \par \uc2  The four-day assembly meeting, which ends later on Friday, was called to discuss political reform and bring forward parliamentary elections to next year from 2002. But protesters say it is a remnant of the autocratic regime of former President Suharto and will not deliver sufficient political change. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the fate of markets next week depends on whether violence and political instability continues after the MPR session is over. Most rupiah dealers squared up their positions and went home early on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Operators were unwilling to take any new positions. Many operators had already gone home before the market closed," one dealer at a foreign bank in Jakarta said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah's strengthening in recent days surprised many operators, who had been long of dollars and were forced into stop-less selling as the week progressed. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said heavy offshore dollar selling in Singapore and Hong Kong had supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the week, and many suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a hand in propping up the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  "It has all the hallmarks of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said the head of treasury at a foreign bank. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been converting $10-15 million a day of aid and loan dollars into rupiah in recent months, giving the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a significant boost in the thin market conditions. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  insists it is not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and says its selling of dollars for rupiah is purely to meet budgetary needs and satisfy the market's dollar demand. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926dubd04kho\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
