\par\par \b \uc2  Rupiah rebounds on offshore dollar selling. \b0\par\par\uc2 432 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 1998\par 05:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Nov 13 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah rebounded in mid-morning trade on Friday as offshore dollar selling helped the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  claw back its losses after an early drop below 8,000 to the dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  By 0340 GMT the rupiah was quoted at 7,750/7,900 to the dollar after earlier falling to 8,100. It closed at 7,750/7,850 in Jakarta on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was also boosted after armed forces chief General Wiranto denied a radio report that he had asked businesses to close. Wiranto said the situation in Jakarta was calm. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was extremely quiet and many players in Jakarta were likely to shut up shop early. \par \par \uc2  "We are just here to square our positions and then go home," said a dealer at a European bank in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  The heavily guarded capital was braced for further unrest after a week of the worst violence seen in Jakarta since mass rioting helped topple former President Suharto in May. \par \par \uc2  Security forces fired tear gas and water cannons on Thursday as thousands of protesters marched on parliament where a special session of Indonesia's highest legislative body, the People's Consultative Assembly (MPR) is being held. \par \par \uc2  Bullets were also fired at the protesters. It was unclear whether they were live round, rubber bullets or blanks. \par \par \uc2  One police officer and one student were killed in the clashes. \par \par \uc2  The MPR is discussing political reforms, but protesters say it is not going far enough to bring democracy to Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  A government official said the release of Indonesia's latest monthly letter of intent to the International Monetary Fund had been delayed to Sunday from Friday. No reason was given for the delay. \par \par \uc2  Offshore dollar selling has been a feature of recent days, helping the rupiah strengthen despite concerns about unrest. Many suspect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a hand in the dollar selling in an effort to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market is dead. The offshore players are not in," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia has been converting $10-15 million a day of aid and loan dollars into rupiah in recent months, giving the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a significant boost in the thin market conditions. \par \par \uc2  But Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  insists it is not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and says its selling of dollars for rupiah is purely to meet budgetary needs and satisfy the market's dollar demand. ((Andrew Marshall, Jakarta newsroom +6221 384 6364; Fax +6221 344 8404, jakarta.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926dubd04jl7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
