\par\par \b \uc2  Rupiah falls through 8,000 after Jakarta clashes. \b0\par\par\uc2 782 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 1998\par 05:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Nov 13 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah fell through 8,000 to the dollar on Friday as fears of more clashes on the streets of the capital and a statement from the armed forces chief telling businesses to close hit sentiment, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  By 0230 GMT the rupiah was trading at 8,100 after closing at 7,750/7,850 in Jakarta on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was extremely quiet and many players in Jakarta were likely to shut up shop early. \par \par \uc2  "We are just here to square our positions and then go home," said a dealer at a European bank in Jakarta. The heavily guarded capital was braced for further unrest after a week of the worst violence seen in Jakarta since mass rioting helped topple former President Suharto in May. \par \par \uc2  Security forces fired rubber bullets, tear gas and water cannons on Thursday as thousands of protesters marched on parliament where a special session of Indonesia's highest legislative body, the People's Consultative Assembly (MPR) is being held. One police officer was killed in the clashes. \par \par \uc2  The MPR is discussing political reforms, but protesters say it is not going far enough to bring democracy to Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  Armed forces chief General Wiranto urged businesses to close for the day and people to stay at home, local radio reported. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had no plans to close and clearing would be unaffected. \par \par \uc2  "Wiranto's statement hit the rupiah, it damaged confidence and there has been a knee-jerk reaction by the rupiah," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  A government official said the release of Indonesia's latest monthly letter of intent to the International Monetary Fund had been delayed to Sunday from Friday. No reason was given for the delay. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no early sign of offshore dollar selling, which has been a feature of recent days and helped the rupiah strengthen earlier in the week despite concerns about unrest. Many suspect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a hand in the dollar selling in an effort to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market is dead. The offshore players are not in," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia has been converting $10-15 million a day of aid and loan dollars into rupiah in recent months, giving the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a significant boost in the thin market conditions. \par \par \uc2  But Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  insists it is not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and says its selling of dollars for rupiah is purely to meet budgetary needs and satisfy the market's dollar demand. ((Andrew Marshall, Jakarta newsroom +6221 384 6364; Fax +6221 344 8404, jakarta.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  The heavily guarded capital was braced for further unrest after a week of the worst violence seen in Jakarta since mass rioting helped topple former President Suharto in May. \par \par \uc2  Security forces fired rubber bullets, tear gas and water cannons on Thursday as thousands of protesters marched on parliament where a special session of Indonesia's highest legislative body, the People's Consultative Assembly (MPR) is being held. One police officer was killed in the clashes. \par \par \uc2  The MPR is discussing political reforms, but protesters say it is not going far enough to bring democracy to Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  Armed forces chief General Wiranto urged businesses to close for the day and people to stay at home, local radio reported. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had no plans to close and clearing would be unaffected. \par \par \uc2  "Wiranto's statement hit the rupiah, it damaged confidence and there has been a knee-jerk reaction by the rupiah," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  A government official said the release of Indonesia's latest monthly letter of intent to the International Monetary Fund had been delayed to Sunday from Friday. No reason was given for the delay. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no early sign of offshore dollar selling, which has been a feature of recent days and helped the rupiah strengthen earlier in the week despite concerns about unrest. Many suspect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a hand in the dollar selling in an effort to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market is dead. The offshore players are not in," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia has been converting $10-15 million a day of aid and loan dollars into rupiah in recent months, giving the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a significant boost in the thin market conditions. \par \par \uc2  But Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  insists it is not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and says its selling of dollars for rupiah is purely to meet budgetary needs and satisfy the market's dollar demand. ((Andrew Marshall, Jakarta newsroom +6221 384 6364; Fax +6221 344 8404, jakarta.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926dubd04j3m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
