\par\par \b \uc2  Rupiah steady, keeps wary eye on assembly clashes. \b0\par\par\uc2 527 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 1998\par 05:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Nov 12 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah was steady in early trade on Thursday, help up by more offshore dollar selling despite growing concerns about clashes on the streets of Jakarta, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar selling started in Hong Kong early this week and was joined by Singapore yesterday and today," said the head of treasury at a foreign bank in Jakarta. "It has all the signs of being \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but it's surprising it's coming from offshore." \par \par \uc2  By 0255 GMT the rupiah was quoted at 7,750/7,850 to the dollar compared with 7,700/7,800 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said many market players had been long of dollars ahead of this week's special session of Indonesia's highest legislative body, the People's Consultative Assembly (MPR). \par \par \uc2  The rupiah's gains this week took many by surprise and triggered stop-loss dollar selling. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people in the market have been caught long and keep having to dump out all the time," the head of treasury said. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a U.S. bank in Jakarta said dollar selling had failed to lift the rupiah on Thursday because of growing nervousness about unrest during the MPR assembly session. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, Indonesian troops fired into the air and baton-charged thousands of students protesting against the special session. \par \par \uc2  "The situation in Jakarta seems to be getting worse, slightly worse," the dealer said. "But the dollar selling has continued in Singapore, so the rupiah is fairly stable." \par \par \uc2  Indonesia has been converting $10-15 million a day of aid and loan dollars into rupiah in recent months, giving the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a significant boost in the thin market conditions - Merrill Lynch has estimated average daily turnover at just $70 million. \par \par \uc2  But Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  insists it is not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and says its selling of dollars for rupiah is purely to meet budgetary needs and satisfy the market's dollar demand. \par \par \uc2  Dealers disagree, however, and believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had a hand in boosting the rupiah this week. \par \par \uc2  International Monetary Fund Asia Pacific Director Hubert Neiss, in Indonesia for a regular monthly review of the country's progress on economic reforms, said on Wednesday there was no reason the rupiah could not strengthen further. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether concerns about the political situation were affecting the market, he said: "Political conditions always affect confidence and confidence affects the market. We hope that confidence will further increase and the rupiah will further strengthen. There is no reason why it shouldn't strengthen." \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia's weekly auction of its one-month SBI certificates on Wednesday resulted in a weighted average interest rate of 51.16 percent against 53.66 percent last week. \par \par \uc2  The bank said the monthly auction of its three-month SBI paper resulted in a weigthed interest rate of 50.49 percent against 58.71 percent last month. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the inverted yield curve showed the market expected interest rates to continue falling. ((Andrew Marshall, Jakarta newsroom +6221 384 6364; Fax +6221 344 8404, jakarta.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926dubc04opq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
