\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Jakarta markets unruffled by street clashes. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Marshall \par \uc2 504 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 1998\par 14:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Nov 11 (Reuters) - Indonesia's markets firmed on Wednesday despite fresh clashes on the streets of Jakarta, with dealers saying unrest during a key meeting of the country's highest legislative body was less severe than had been feared. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah vaulted the 8,000 level and ended at 7,700/7,800 to the dollar after closing at 8,100/8,200 on Tuesday. Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was boosted by offshore dollar selling, and some suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a hand in supporting the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Shares also advanced, with the stock market climbing 1.6 percent to 363 points, led by companies with heavy foreign debts which benefited from the rupiah's relative strength. \par \par \uc2  "Rupiah gains helped indebted blue chips...while optimism increased that the session of the MPR will run smoothly," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The People's Consultative Assembly (MPR) has convened for a four-day special session to discuss political reforms and bring forward parliamentary elections to next year from 2002. \par \par \uc2  But market attention is focused less on the political machinations inside parliament than on events on the streets outside, where student protesters who believe the pace of reform is too slow have clashed with troops and civilian security volunteers brandishing sticks and bamboo spears. \par \par \uc2  Troops fired in the air on Wednesday and charged with batons to block thousands of students trying to march on the parliament building. But there were no reports of serious injuries. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said markets remained on edge ahead of the end of the assembly on Friday, but there was some relief that the severe violence some had forecast had not materialised. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was also boosted by expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would step in to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it began to slide. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia has been converting $10-15 million a day of aid and loan dollars into rupiah in recent months, giving the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a significant boost in the thin market conditions. \par \par \uc2  Dealers say they expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step up the amount of dollars being converted into rupiah if nervousness about the assembly meeting hits the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was widely thought to have been behind offshore dollar selling on Tuesday and Wednesday, which triggered stop-loss dollar selling by other operators. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  insists it is not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and says its selling of dollars for rupiah is purely to meet budgetary needs and satisfy the market's dollar demand. \par \par \uc2  International Monetary Fund Asia Pacific Director Hubert Neiss, in Indonesia for a regular monthly review of the country's progress on economic reforms, said there was no reason the rupiah could not strengthen further. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether concerns about the political situation were affecting the market, he said: "Political conditions always affect confidence and confidence affects the market. We hope that confidence will further increase and the rupiah will further strengthen. There is no reason why it shouldn't strengthen." \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926dubb04r82\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
