\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia rupiah climbs despite assembly nerves. \b0\par\par\uc2 616 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 1998\par 10:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Nov 10 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah gained ground in afternoon trade on Tuesday on dollar selling both offshore and in Jakarta, with some dealers saying they suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a hand in boosting the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  By 0815 GMT the rupiah was quoted at 8,000/8,200 to the dollar, compared with 8,500/8,600 in early trade and 8,425/8,525 at the close of Jakarta dealings on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "We've seen dollar selling in Hong Kong, mainly through interbank players," said a dealer at a U.S. bank in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  "Some suspect that some of the selling in Hong Kong is on behalf of Bank Indonesia." \par \par \uc2  Another dealer said there were rumours that Bank Indonesia had been selling more dollars than usual but this had not been confirmed. \par \par \uc2  "German names have been selling, but we don't know what's behind that," said the dealer at a European bank in Jakarta. "We don't know whether there is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we still haven't been able to substantiate that." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's highest legislative body, the People's Consultative Assembly (MPR), began a four-day special session to ratify the country's eletion timetable and discuss laws which President B.J. Habibie has promised will make a more democratic Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  But markets are focused less on the political machinations inside Jakarta's parliament building than on events on the streets outside - where clashes are feared between pro-reform protesters spearheaded by students and Moslem groups armed with sticks who have vowed to protect the assembly. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia has been converting $10-15 million a day of aid and loan dollars into rupiah in recent months, giving the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a significant boost in the thin market conditions - Merrill Lynch has estimated average daily turnover in the rupiah at around just $70 million. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said earlier this week they expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step up the amount of dollars being converted into rupiah if nervousness about the assembly meeting hit the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Indonesian state banks were aggressively bidding rupiah higher, as in recent days, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  insists it is not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and says its selling of dollars for rupiah is purely to meet budgetary needs and satisfy the market's dollar demand. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was also boosted on Tuesday by comments from International Monetary Fund first deputy managing director Stanley Fischer, who said in a speech in Australia there was no reason why the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could not move back to 7,000 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We guess that with the amount of inflation that has happened so far this year...if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to stop in the 6,500-7,000 or so (range) you would find yourself with a devaluation in real terms roughly similar to that in the other crisis countries of somewhere between 25 and 35 percent," he said. \par \par \uc2  No major trouble was reported at the start of the assembly session, although thousands of government supporters, many with sharpened bamboo sticks, rallied near the parliament building, occasionally attacking passers-by. \par \par \uc2  They later moved off without any major incident. \par \par \uc2  Reuters reporters at the scene said government security forces had trouble keeping the demonstrators under control and that nearby roads were closed, including the toll road to the airport. \par \par \uc2  Thousands of students gathered at a central Jakarta university campus for a protest rally but said they had not yet decided whether to march on parliament because of the danger of clashes with government supporters. ((Andrew Marshall, Jakarta newsroom +6221 384 6364; Fax +6221 344 8404, jakarta.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926duba04vly\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
