\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA FOCUS - Chronology of Asia's financial crisis. \b0\par\par\uc2 798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1998\par 04:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 3 (Reuters) - The following is a chronology of Asia's financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  1997 \par \par \uc2  Jan 23 - Leading South Korean steel maker Hanbo Steel Corp defaults on loans, the first of a string of major corporate failures in 1997. \par \par \uc2  May 14-15 - Thailand's baht \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes under attack by speculators who decided Thailand's slowing economy and political instability meant it was time to sell. Thailand and Singapore jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the baht. \par \par \uc2  The Philippines is affected. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises the overnight rate 1-percentage points to 13 percent and dumps dollars. \par \par \uc2  June 19 - Amnuay Viravan, staunchly against devaluing the baht, resigns as Thai finance minister. Resignation has immediate impact in the Philippines, where the overnight deposit rate rises to 15 percent. \par \par \uc2  June 27 - Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspends \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of 16 cash-strapped finance companies and orders them to submit merger or consolidation plans. \par \par \uc2  June 30 - Thai Prime Minister Chavalit Yongchaiyudh assures the nation in a televised address there will be no devaluation of the baht. \par \par \uc2  July 2 - After a failed defence of the Thai baht, Bangkok floats its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and calls on the IMF for "technical assistance". \par \par \uc2  The baht is effectively devalued by about 15-20 percent. \par \par \uc2  July 8 - Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Negara has to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively to defend the ringgit. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the rupiah starts to crumble. \par \par \uc2  July 14 - The IMF offers the Philippines almost $1.1 billion in financial support. \par \par \uc2  July 24 - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  meltdown. The rupiah, baht, ringgit and peso slump as confidence in the region deteriorates. \par \par \uc2  August 11 - In Tokyo, the IMF unveils a rescue package for Thailand including loans of $16 billion from the IMF and Asian nations. \par \par \uc2  August 14 - Indonesia abolishes its system of a managed exchange rate. The rupiah plunges. \par \par \uc2  August 20 - IMF approves a $3.9 billion credit for Thailand. Brunei later adds $500 million to the bail-out package, bringing it to $17.2 billion. \par \par \uc2  Sept 20 - Malaysian Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad tells delegates of IMF/World Bank conference in Hong Kong that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading is immoral. \par \par \uc2  Oct 8 - Indonesia says it will ask the IMF for financial assistance. \par \par \uc2  Oct 20-23 - The Hong Kong stock market suffers its heaviest drubbing ever, shedding nearly a quarter of its value in four days on uncertainty over the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  Around this time the South Korean won also begins to slump in value. \par \par \uc2  Oct 27 - Asian jitters spill over on to world stock markets. The Dow plunges 554 points, its largest single-day point loss ever. \par \par \uc2  Oct 31 - Indonesia's IMF package is unveiled. It provides for as much as $40 billion in aid, although the front-line defence is $23 billion. \par \par \uc2  Nov 1 - Indonesia shuts down 16 troubled banks. \par \par \uc2  Nov 5 - IMF approves a $10 billion loan for Indonesia as part of the massive international package. \par \par \uc2  Nov 17 - Korea abandons its defence of the won, which rapidly falls to 1,000 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dec 4 - IMF approves a $21 billion loan for South Korea, part of a bail-out package that will total nearly $60 billion. \par \par \uc2  Dec 8 - Thailand closes 56 of 58 previously suspended finance companies. \par \par \uc2  1998 \par \par \uc2  Jan 8 - Representatives of the IMF and South Korea agree to a 90-day rollover of short-term debt. \par \par \uc2  Jan 12 - Peregrine, one of Asia's largest independent investment banks, collapses under a mountain of bad loans to Indonesian borrowers. Shares fall sharply in Hong Kong, China and Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Jan 15 - IMF managing director Michel Camdessus and President Suharto sign an agreement strengthening economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  Jan 22 - The rupiah collapses. It is dealt at 17,000 to the dollar at one stage. Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  and drives the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back to end at 11,800. \par \par \uc2  Jan 27 - Indonesia announces a temporary freeze on debt servicing. \par \par \uc2  Jan 28 - International creditor banks and the South Korean government say they have agreed on a plan to exchange $24 billion of short-term debt for new loans maturing in one, two and three years. \par \par \uc2  Jan 30 - U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan warns Asia's financial crisis could spread. \par \par \uc2  Feb 14 - The IMF threatens to cut off the bail-out fund for Indonesia over its plans to introduce a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board. \par \par \uc2  Feb 26 - Indonesia's borrowers and lenders begin negotiations on rescheduling at least $70 billion in private off-shore debt. \par \par \uc2  March 1 - Indonesian President Suharto says reforms prescribed by the IMF have not worked. \par \par \uc2  March 4 - The IMF approves release of the third tranche payment from Thailand's package and commends the Thai government for its economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  March 6 - IMF says its board will not discuss Indonesia's economic reform programme before April, effectively delaying a $3 billion payment initially expected by March 15. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du930223a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
