\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Indonesia wants sound banks by year-end. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nick Edwards \par \uc2 823 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 1998\par 13:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Aug 25 (Reuters) - Indonesian Finance Minister Bambang Subianto said on Tuesday he wanted a sound banking system in place in the country by the end of the year to bring interest and exchange rates under control and restore business confidence. \par \par \uc2  "We want the sector healthier by the year end to get them (the banks) supporting the economy for 1999," Subianto told a capital markets conference in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  Restoring the health of the ailing banking sector is a pre-requisite if Indonesia was to restore its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rate to sensible, sustainable levels, bring down soaring interest and inflation rates and return liquidity to a cash-strangled economy. \par \par \uc2  "Our main objective is to have a banking system in place to be able to support the economy properly," Subianto said. \par \par \uc2  Bank reform is a key tenet of the $41.2 billion rescue package prescribed by the International Monetary Fund for Indonesia, which is in the throes of its worst economic crisis in more than 30 years, threatening massive social unrest in its wake. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the perilous health of the banks has been the major factor behind the collapse in confidence in Indonesia, whose corporations are struggling to dig themselves out from under a foreign debt mountain of some $80 billion. \par \par \uc2  Subianto last week took serious steps on the road to reconstructing the sector, freezing, merging or nationalising 11 ailing banks. The minister said he intended to resolve the fate of 38 others under the watch of the Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency (IBRA) in the very near future. \par \par \uc2  "In the coming months you will see decisions coming on the other 38," he told the conference. \par \par \uc2  Subianto's words provided fresh stimulus for the strengthening rupiah \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which analysts say could be heading for the 10,000 level to the U.S. dollar by the year end. \par \par \uc2  It broke decisively through 12,000 a week ago and has tested 11,000 several times since. At 0831 GMT on Tuesday, the rupiah was quoted at 10,950/11,125 per dollar. It was around 2,500 to the dollar before the economic crisis hit in July last year. \par \par \uc2  Sjahril Sabirin, governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank Indonesia, told conference delegates: "This morning the rupiah strengthened above 11,000 to the dollar. We are winning!" \par \par \uc2  The market disagreed with him however. "The rupiah was bid below 11,000 yes, but it was only screen play. The best it has done was 11,250," a dealer with a U.S. bank told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Another dealer said: "It was bid through 11,000, but nobody took it. The resistance seems to be at 11,200 at the moment." Technical factors point to a 10,000 level to the U.S. dollar by the year end, mainly as a result of some $10 billion of loans and aid due for injection in the country in the fiscal year ending in March 1999. \par \par \uc2  These dollars will be converted into rupiah for spending on infrastructure and other projects. \par \par \uc2  Stabilising the exchange rate would also be a big help in slashing choking interest rates, which Bank Indonesia set at a weighted average of 70.14 percent for its one month paper in auctions last week. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  managing director, Miranda Gultom said tackling interest and exchange rates were the priority to get business costs back under control, although there was no avoiding the short term pain of high rates. \par \par \uc2  "We have to stabilise the rupiah - there is no question about it," she told the conference, adding that tight monetary policy was still needed "even though high interest rates will hamper our effort to start on the road to recovery". \par \par \uc2  She said the return to a managed exchange rate system could also be on the agenda for the future. \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a system of managed exchange rates through an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band until last August when the rupiah was freed after the financial crisis hit the region. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders in Jakarta said such a move would be welcome. \par \par \uc2  "It would be a good idea. We would be better able to plan our topside and our bottom and draw up stop loss programmes," said a dealer with a European bank. \par \par \uc2  Added impetus for the rupiah against the dollar should come if the government's reforms began to restore the confidence that has evaporated in the economy domestically and internationally. \par \par \uc2  Dennis de Tray, the World Bank's chief representative in Indonesia, said the best strategy to restore confidence was to set quick win reform targets that beat market expectations. \par \par \uc2  "Actions, not words, restore confidence. You can promise the moon, but you have to deliver it," he told delegates, adding that the government had tried to introduce reform measures in the past but they had failed live up to what was anticipated. \par \par \uc2  "You have to stay ahead of the expectation curve," he said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8p01jx0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
