\par\par \b \uc2  Rupiah ends lower on north Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 786 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 1998\par 12:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Aug 7 (Reuters) - The rupiah ended slightly lower against the dollar on Friday following jitters in the Japanese yen, Chinese yuan and the Hong Kong dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said dollar short-covering ahead of a long weekend in Singapore also helped push the rupiah lower. \par \par \uc2  Spot rupiah ended at 12,850 after hovering between 12,650 and 13,000 through the day after opening at 12,675/12,775. The rupiah ended on Thursday at 12,650/12,750 in Jakarta and at 12,750/12,850 in New York. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah however ended the week higher compared with 13,125 last Friday on improved sentiment due to anticipation of inflows from international donors. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said sustained weakness in the yen had concerned the market over how long could the yuan withstand pressures. \par \par \uc2  Fears the yuan could be devalued and an attack on the Hong Kong dollar kept pressure on the regionals. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment was rather mixed in the afternoon. I think it's mainly due to the yen, yuan and Hong Kong dollars," one said. \par \par \uc2  The American bank dealer said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen selling small amounts of dollars, but that failed to prevent the rupiah inching closer to the 13,000 level. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said operators were also concerned over rumours of possible unrest in the run-up to the celebration of Indonesia's Independence Day on August 17. \par \par \uc2  "People are a bit worried about the rumours. I think some will take precautionary steps," one local bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Jakarta's police chief Nugroho Djajusman said security forces were on guard against any new unrest in the capital, amid rumours that fresh riots were being planned. \par \par \uc2  Nugroho said the city's residents should also organise their own security measures against crime and the threat of unrest. \par \par \uc2  But he said that rumours circulating in the city that there would be violence on August 17 were unfounded. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the new letter of chief economics minister Ginandjar Kartasasmita to the IMF following recent review of the country's structural and adjustment programmes announced on Friday had no effect on the market. \par \par \uc2  They said the substantive points were in the banking sector, with the government specifying what actions were to be taken in the next few weeks. \par \par \uc2  "It has been long expected. There is no impact in the market," one chief treasurer with a local bank said. \par \par \uc2  Chief economics minister Ginandjar Kartasasmita has said Indonesia would use international aid in dollars to buy rupiah in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the coming months. \par \par \uc2  Ginandjar said the ecomomy's main source of trouble was a lack of international confidence reflected in the level of the rupiah compared to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  He said the government aimed to raise the rupiah to about 10,000 to the dollar by the end of March 1999. \par \par \uc2  He said that in the course of the coming months there would be continuous purchases of the rupiah in the market, which was not the same as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but should help. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said liquidity eased in the afternoon after a tight start due to carry-forward effect from Wednesday. They said overnight money for state banks eased to 53 percent. \par \par \uc2  In the morning, overnight money for state and foreign banks hovered around 63 and 65 percent, for local banks around 70 to 75 percent and for banks under supervision around 100 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said it might be due to carry-forward impact from a higher-than-expected allocation of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  weekly auction \par \par \uc2  They said SBIs rates were much higher than interbank rates, allowing operators to arbitrage. \par \par \uc2  The banks' weekly auction of SBIs resulted in an absorption of 14,151 billion rupiah at a weighted average interest rate of 68.33 percent, accounting for 87 percent of the incoming bids. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had targeted mopping up 13 trillion rupiah for its 28-day paper or treasury bills. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said swap rates were little changed in the afternoon. In Singapore, spot/next swaps were at 17/19, one-week at 130/140, one-month at 600/620, two at 1,070/1,170, three at 1,550/1,650, six at 2,900/3,100, nine at 4,100/4,400 and one-year at 5,400/5,700 points. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday added rupiah \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates for two more tenors in mid-morning, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  paid 51 percent for overnight money and 53 percent for four-and five-day money. The bank had earlier only set 53 percent for six-day money. \par \par \uc2  - Jakarta newsroom (6221) 384-6364; Fax (6221) 344-8404 \par \par \uc2  - Email: jakarta.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8702e1d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
