\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesian rupiah holds on gains as yen recovers. \b0\par\par\uc2 690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 1998\par 12:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Aug 5 (Reuters) - The rupiah held on to its gains against the dollar in late trading on Wednesday on the back of a recovering yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said the rupiah could renew its test on the 12,500 resistance level if the yen sustained its recovery. Some dealers were sceptical because of the gloomy economic outlook. \par \par \uc2  Spot rupiah ended in Jakarta at 12,750 after hovering between 12,650 and 12,900 through the day. The rupiah ended on Tuesday at 12,950 in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment is still strong on the rupiah. But a lot depends on the yen," one European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yen seemed to given a firmer tone to regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  following the steep sell-off in U.S. stocks overnight, dragging stock markets lower across the region. \par \par \uc2  "I think the U.S. economy is not immune to troubles in Asia. How do you want to sell if we can't absorb their goods? People are looking at the mark and sterling now," one American bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Deputy U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers set a bearish tone for Asian markets by saying the financial troubles in the region might be far from over. \par \par \uc2  "I would say we have a long way to go in working through these problems," Summers told the National Governors' Association, when asked about Asia's financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  "But I think with a strong response, and particularly if the Japanese take what I think are crucial steps for them, I think this can remain very much a contained situation, and that there is no inherent reason why it needs...to pose a threat to the basic momentum of an American economic expansion." \par \par \uc2  He said the economies of South Korea and Thailand were under more control, but the situation in Indonesia was more serious. \par \par \uc2  "Relative to six months ago, I think that there is a sense of the situation coming under more control in Korea and in Thailand, relative to what most observers would have expected six months ago," Summers said. \par \par \uc2  "I think that largely for political rather than economic reasons, the situation in Indonesia is more serious than it appeared six months ago," Summers added. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yield curve remained on an inverted trend, with supports in shorter-dates below one-month for arbitrage and funding purposes while the longer-end tended to soften. \par \par \uc2  One foreign bank dealer said an easing trend in longer-term rates suggested improved rupiah expectations. \par \par \uc2  In Singapore, spot/next swaps were at 52/56, one-week at 125/135, one-month at 550/600, two at 950/1,050, three at 1,440/1,540, six at 2,750/2,900, nine at 3,950/4,150 and one-year at 5,000/5,300 points. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia's weekly auction of SBI certificates resulted in an absorption of 14,151 billion rupiah at a weighted average interest rate of 68.33 percent, accounting for 87 percent of the incoming bids. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had targeted mopping up 13 trillion rupiah for its 28-day paper or treasury bills. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the higher than expected rupiah contraction allowed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to relax its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  "It's an encouraging result after last week's debut. People are getting used to it," one Japanese bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The dealer however said the new system could endanger small banks' balance sheets because depositors could switch deposits to risk-free treasury bills with high returns. \par \par \uc2  The first weekly 13 trillion rupiah auction of SBI certificates last week resulted in an absorption of 6.969 trillion rupiah at a weighted average interest rate of 65.16 percent, accounting for 91 percent of the incoming bids. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday set rupiah \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates for four tenors. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  paid 51 percent for overnight money, 52 percent for two-day money, 53 percent for five-and six-day money and 59 percent for seven-day money. \par \par \uc2  - Jakarta newsroom (6221) 384-6364; Fax (6221) 344-8404 \par \par \uc2  - Email: jakarta.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du8501v1t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
