\par\par \b \uc2  Rupiah strengthens, shrugs off riot anniversary. \b0\par\par\uc2 607 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 1998\par 12:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, July 27 (Reuters) - The rupiah strengthened against the dollar on Monday, shrugging off the anniversary of 1996 riots in Jakarta that went off peacefully, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said the rupiah instead was given a boost by optimistic expectations of a meeting of donor countries on Indonesia in Paris this week. \par \par \uc2  Spot rupiah crossed the 14,000 resistance level to a high of 13,800 before ending in Jakarta at 13,950 after hovering between 14,050 and 14,300 last week. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers linked the rise to anticipation of increased loans from the Paris meeting to help cover the budget deficit. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said stop-loss dollar selling was triggered after the rupiah touched the 14,000 level. The rupiah was hovering around 14,200 level in the morning. \par \par \uc2  "We see some dollar selling from a European name. We don't know what was the reason. That helped boost the rupiah in a thin market," an American bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The Consultative Group for Indonesia (CGI) meeting in Paris on Tuesday and Wednesday will decide on the amount of loans to the country. Planning Minister Boediono said amount was likely to be over the 1997 figure of $5.3 billion. \par \par \uc2  The government has said it had already secured $6.2 billion in budget support from donor countries. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, a spokeswoman of the Development Cooperation Ministry said on Sunday that the Dutch government had replied positively to a request by President B.J. Habibie to resume development funding for Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was calm despite initial jitters over plans to mark the second anniversary of riots in Jakarta following the takeover of the headquarters of the Indonesian Democratic Party (PDI) after the ousting of Megawati Sukarnoputri as party leader. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the rupiah had held up well despite fears of possible unrest connected with the anniversary. \par \par \uc2  Serious riots had followed the takeover of the PDI offices in central Jakarta in July 1996 by a government-backed faction that had deposed Megawati as PDI chief. \par \par \uc2  However, the anniversary passed peacefully. \par \par \uc2  Thousands of troops and police had been placed on alert across Jakarta after authorities banned a rally in the city centre fearing outbreaks of violence. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said operators were also looking forward for the implementation on Wednesday of a new auction system. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to start with the auction of one-month paper on Wednesday and later expand to a full range of maturities as agreed with the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said the move would be the first step by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in switching to an open auction system after almost two decades of setting interest rates administratively. \par \par \uc2  In the afternoon, Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  added \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in overnight money into its monetary programme, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offered to buy overnight money at 48 percent, apparently to meet its monetary target. They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the morning was only offering three tenors in its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity. \par \par \uc2  The bank paid 53 percent for three-day money, 60 percent for 10-day and 61 percent for 17-day. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said operators were seen unloading forwards after spot rupiah staged a recovery in the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  In Singapore, spot/next swaps were at 15/16, one-week at 80/110, one-month at 500/540, two at 960/1,040, three at 1,400/1,500, six at 2,800/3,000, nine at 4,100/4,500 and one-year at 5,400/5,900. \par \par \uc2  - Jakarta newsroom (6221) 384-6364; Fax (6221) 344-8404 \par \par \uc2  - Email: jakarta.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7r0048n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
