\par\par \b \uc2  Chronology of Indonesia's financial crisis. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,070 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 1998\par 07:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, June 25 (Reuters) - A chronology of Indonesia's financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  1997 \par \par \uc2  Jul 8 - The rupiah starts to crumble. Jakarta widens its rupiah trading band to 12 from eight percent. \par \par \uc2  Aug 14 - Jakarta abolishes managed exchange rate system. The rupiah sinks further. \par \par \uc2  Oct 6 - Rupiah hits a low of 3,845. \par \par \uc2  Oct 8 - Indonesia says it will ask the International Monetary Fund for assistance. \par \par \uc2  Oct 31 - IMF package unveiled with more than $40 billion in aid. \par \par \uc2  1998 \par \par \uc2  Jan 6 - Indonesia unveils its 1998/99 budget projecting four percent economic growth. The rupiah loses half its value against the dollar over five days, breaking through 10,000. \par \par \uc2  Jan 10 - President Suharto announces the delay or review of 15 infrastructure projects. \par \par \uc2  Jan 14 - IMF Managing Director Michel Camdessus says the IMF is asking Indonesia to sign a new letter of intent. \par \par \uc2  Jan 15 - Camdessus and Suharto sign an agreement strengthening economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  Jan 22 - The rupiah hits a low of 17,000. Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it recovers to 11,800. \par \par \uc2  Jan 23 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  presents a revised budget expecting zero growth in fiscal 1998 and 20 percent inflation, but which assumes the rupiah will be at 5,000 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Jan 27 - Indonesia announces a temporary freeze on debt servicing. \par \par \uc2  Jan 28 - Mobs attack shops owned by ethnic Chinese in Central Java over rising food prices. \par \par \uc2  Feb 4 - Indonesian government gives a lukewarm response to a proposal by U.S. economist Steve Hanke to peg the rupiah under a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system. \par \par \uc2  Feb 6 - Chief debt negotiator Radius Prawiro says Indonesia's total foreign debt is $137.424 billion. \par \par \uc2  Feb 9 - Signature campaign to replace Suharto begins. \par \par \uc2  Feb 11 - Finance Minister Mar'ie Muhammad says government is preparing for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board. The rupiah rises to 7,200 to the dollar. Police detain 140 people after protests in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  Feb 12 - Ruling Golkar party names Research and Technology Minister B.J. Habibie as one of its vice presidential candidates. \par \par \uc2  Feb 13 - Suharto tells President Clinton the IMF is failing to stem Indonesia's financial crisis. Clinton objects to Jakarta's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board plans. \par \par \uc2  Feb 14 - IMF threatens to halt its bail-out fund. Police detain 154 people in riots over rising prices that leave at least three dead. \par \par \uc2  Feb 17 - Suharto fires \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Soedradjad Djiwandono for opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board. Djiwandono is replaced by U.S.-trained economist Sjahril Sabirin. \par \par \uc2  Feb 18 - Habibie wins armed forces' backing for vice-presidency. \par \par \uc2  Feb 19 - U.S. Commerce Secretary William Daley and German Finance Minister Waigel insist Indonesia must follow IMF-prescribed reforms. \par \par \uc2  Feb 20 - Finance Minister Mar'ie Muhammad says Indonesia so far faced no problem in meeting state foreign debts. \par \par \uc2  Feb 26 - Indonesian companies hold their first formal meeting with a steering committee of lenders to resolve the country's estimated $74 billion in private debt. \par \par \uc2  Mar 6 - IMF delays disbursement of second $3 billion tranche to Indonesia due March 15. \par \par \uc2  Mar 10 - Suharto re-elected president for a seventh five-year term by the 1,000 member People's Consultative Assembly (MPR), as thousands of students hold protest rallies. \par \par \uc2  Mar 11 - Suharto sworn in. Habibie elected vice-president. \par \par \uc2  Mar 16 - Suharto swears in 36 new cabinet ministers, including daughter Siti Hardiyanti Rukmana and a golfing buddy, as students and riot police clash at the National University in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  Mar 20 - Indonesia says decision has been made to drop the idea of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board for the time being. \par \par \uc2  Mar 26 - The Jakarta post reports that unemployment has risen to about 8.7 million people, or 10 percent of the workforce. \par \par \uc2  Mar 27 - Indonesia proposes to the IMF a formula to resolve its huge private debt, drawing on the Mexican "Ficorca" model. \par \par \uc2  Apr 2 - IMF and Indonesia agree new figures for the fiscal 1998/99 budget, including a decline of five percent in GDP, inflation of 45 percent and a budget deficit of 3.5 percent. IMF warns Suharto to show commitment on tough reforms or lose its support. \par \par \uc2  Apr 4 - Government suspends licences of seven banks. Seven other banks placed under the supervision of the Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency. Suharto relatives and friends are known to control some of them, or hold large equity stakes. \par \par \uc2  Apr 6 - Finance Minster Faud Bawazier says the government will set up two state companies to handle the assets of liquidated banks and insure depositors. \par \par \uc2  Apr 8 - The IMF says a new agreement on economic reforms will be closely monitored. Indonesia revises its fiscal 1998/99 (April 1) economic growth expectation to minus four percent and its expected budget deficit to 3.2 percent of GDP. It forecasts inflation for the fiscal year of 17 percent. \par \par \uc2  Apr 16 - Indonesia reaches a framework agreement with banks over corporate debts. \par \par \uc2  Apr 22 - Indonesia announces it has met all reform deadlines agreed with the IMF. The rupiah rises to 7,800. \par \par \uc2  May 4 - IMF resumes its stalled lending programme to Indonesia with payment of a $1 billion instalment. \par \par \uc2  May 5 - Riots break out in the Sumatran city of Medan. The violence over several days results in at least six deaths. \par \par \uc2  May 13 - Riots spread to Jakarta and the shooting of at least four student protesters there pushes the rupiah to around 10,000. \par \par \uc2  May 14 - Savage rioting engulfs Jakarta. Suharto cuts short a state visit to Egypt. Later estimates put the total dead at around 1,200. \par \par \uc2  May 16 - Suharto announces plans to reshuffle cabinet. \par \par \uc2  May 18 - Rupiah trading resumes, quoted at 12,600. \par \par \uc2  May 19 - Thousands of students occupy parliament. Suharto says he will step down after seeing through constitutional reforms. \par \par \uc2  May 20 - The IMF says it will not be paying its next $1 billion instalment on June 4. \par \par \uc2  May 21 - President Suharto resigns after 32 years in power. Vice President Habibie sworn in as president. \par \par \uc2  May 30 - IMF's Hubert Neiss concludes four days of talks with Indonesian officials. Neiss says he has no reason to doubt economists' forecasts the Indonesian economy could contract by 10 percent or more in 1998. \par \par \uc2  Jun 4 - International banks agree to reschedule Indonesia's corporate debt mountain of some $80 billion. \par \par \uc2  June 15 - The rupiah trades at around 14,700. Neiss says the target of 6,000 will be reset. \par \par \uc2  June 23 - Bank Indonesia says that in the third week of June it had continued to meet IMF performance criteria. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6p0249y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
