\par\par \b \uc2  Bk Indonesia Gov -3: Rate Of IDR6,000 To Dollar "Realistic" \b0\par\par\uc2 224 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 1998\par 12:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Asked to comment on Indonesia's top economic minister Ginandjar Kartasasmita's prediction earlier Monday that the rupiah would stabilize around IDR6,000 to the U.S. dollar by the first quarter of 1999, Sabirin said that "it was very realistic to have the rupiah at IDR6,000" to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  He said the fall of the rupiah was a "problem of confidence" and that before the news of the demonstrations he would have expected the rupiah to rise further against the dollar to below IDR8,000, especially after the agreement with the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  But the governor said the impact of the riots, which he said the government had mostly contained, on the rupiah would be temporary. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading around IDR9,300 to the dollar as he spoke. \par \par \uc2  As for support for the rupiah by the Bank of Japan by selling dollars for rupiah, which occured last autumn, he said he hadn't raised the issue with the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  Sabirin said he didn't expect to discuss the topic during a meeting Tuesday with the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, Masaru Hayami, reiterating that restoring confidence in \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was more important than \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He described the planned meeting as a courtesy call. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 11-05-98 \par \par \uc2  1039GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du5b03xxu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
