\par\par \b \uc2  Japan reserve policy won't soon be altered by euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tatsuo Ito \par \uc2 601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 1998\par 08:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 11 (Reuters) - Despite euphoria at the impending birth of the euro, Japan's monetary authorities say they do not intend to jump at the chance to boost holdings of the new European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon after its debut in January 1999. \par \par \uc2  Japan, holder of the world's largest foreign reserves, is cautious about making snap decisions to reshuffle components of its reserves, now denominated in U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  "The euro will be officially launched with 11 European nations. However, there's much uncertainty along the way as to how the euro will fare, what character it will possess and how the unified European financial market will look," said a senior official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Japan will take steps after all these factors become clearer," he said. \par \par \uc2  The European Union (EU) held a landmark summit on May 1 and 2 in Brussels, agreeing to launch the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January 1999 with 11 members, and appointing the first president of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB). \par \par \uc2  One monetary source said sound macroeconomic policy in European nations would be in the interests of the Japanese government, while integrated European financial markets free from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks could benefit Japanese firms operating there. \par \par \uc2  But he and analysts said there was uncertainty over whether tight monetary and fiscal policy by European nations would be maintained in concert after the euro was introduced. \par \par \uc2  Germany and the Netherlands could favor tight monetary policies, while Spain and Italy may not accept them, depending on the economic situation. \par \par \uc2  Still, some foreign central banks already see the euro in a positive light. Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said last week it would shift one billion marks into euro holdings immediately after it was launched. \par \par \uc2  Japanese private investors have been shifting some investment into European bonds and equities in the belief that the euro will have a smooth launch, while trying to avoid excessive exposure to U.S. asset markets, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Regarding the possibility of Japanese authorities' increasing euro holdings, however, Taisuke Tanaka, strategist of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Credit Suisse said: "In the short-term period it isn't possible. But on a long-term horizon it is." \par \par \uc2  Some monetary sources agreed. \par \par \uc2  "If Japan comes to realise that stabilising the euro is important to the stability of the yen, Japan would hold it or increase euro holdings to prepare for cases of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said the monetary source. \par \par \uc2  Analysts estimated over 90 percent of Japan's $205.758 billion reserves outstanding was denominated in U.S. dollars, and the rest was in European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the mark and sterling. \par \par \uc2  Japan also began holding the \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  rupiah last November as a result of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore and Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The action, aimed at propping up the value of the rupiah against the dollar, was based on Japanese authorities' judgment that Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability would be important to Japan's economy and firms operating there, monetary officials said. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings by the Finance Ministry were likely to be converted into the euro automatically after 1999. \par \par \uc2  But most said no shift to the euro at the expense of the dollar would take place because of wider economic and political relations with the United States. \par \par \uc2  Credit Suisse's Tanaka said: "Besides economic relations with the United States, political incentives could influence Japan to hold the bulk of reserves in the dollar." ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 1396 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5b05ntu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
