\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia Boosts Interest Rates, But  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Continues To Plunge \b0\par\par\uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 1998\par 09:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Indonesia -(Dow Jones)- Bank Indonesia Governor Sjahril Sabirin announced an across-the-board increase in interest rates Thursday to boost the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the rupiah failed to respond amid a wave of selling prompted by fears of escalating social unrest. \par \par \uc2  Sabirin said the rate increase was meant "to support the rupiah and to curb inflation, which remains high." Following the announcement, overnight lending rates were increased to 48% from 44%; two-day rates to 49% from 45%; and three-to-six-day rates to 50% from 46%. \par \par \uc2  The move came in the face of massive unrest over government-mandated price increases as part of an International Monetary Fund bailout of the Indonesian economy. Fears that rioting that resulted in six deaths in the city of Medan Wednesday would worsen were mostly to blame for a plunge in the rupiah to 9,620 rupiah to the dollar Thursday from Wednesday's close of 9,050. \par \par \uc2  The IMF, which is overseeing a vital bailout package for Indonesia, forced the government to slash generous subsidies on food, fuel and other necessities that have aided poor Indonesians for years. Thousands of people protested by burning cars, tossing rocks at police and attacking hundreds of Chinese-owned stores. \par \par \uc2  Stocks, meanwhile, climbed 5.3% on the Jakarta Stock Exchange following their 4.7% plunge Wednesday. Analysts attributed the increase to arbitrage buying in telecommunications stocks. \par \par \uc2  The IMF disbursed $1 billion in aid to the Indonesian government Monday. Originally, the agency had planned to disburse $3 billion in aid in mid-March but suspended the program during a protracted fight with Indonesian authorities. Now, the remaining $2 billion will be disbursed over the next two months if Indonesia demonstrates its will to comply with the program. \par \par \uc2  The IMF program will free up other loans for the battered Indonesian economy, which began to unravel with the collapse of the rupiah last August. The World Bank and Asian Development Bank are each expected to approve loans of about $500 million over the next few weeks. \par \par \uc2  Sjahril said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  currently believes high interest rates are more effective in stabilizing the rupiah than market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He wouldn't, however, rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in the future. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's director Miranda Gultom added that despite of criticisms on the effectiveness of the rate rise to support the rupiah, the last rate rise in April enticed capital inflows. It showed by the fact that that the value of government securities purchased by foreigners rose to 14.3 trillion rupiah as of April 30 compared to only 1.9 trillion rupiah April 1, Miranda added. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the country's military commander said his troops are forming a team to assist in the advancement of the International Monetary Fund-backed economic program, although he stated that any reforms would be implemented "gradually and constitutionally." The army also sent reinforcements to the riot-torn city of Medan, and offered an olive branch to protesting Indonesian students. \par \par \uc2  Armed Forces General Commander Wiranto stated that the head of the military's faction in parliament, Syarwan Hamid, will be appointed to lead the team which that will be charged with advancing reforms. He also said that the body will include the military's general chief of staff, Fachrul Razi. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Wiranto urged student activists to desist from taking protests off campus for fear of "hurting national stability." He instead invited the students serve as a moral and intellectual force in support of reform. \par \par \uc2  The commander also said the military would establish a framework to put economic and political reforms on the national agenda, an apparent concession to student groups that have been calling for political change. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du5700r0w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
